Maya: Why François Chollet says software companies still aren't making higher profits even though agents write almost all the code, plus Eric Siu's outreach bot that prepares drafts every day without ever pressing send.

32
00:03:30,153 --> 00:03:30,612
Alex: Three.

33
00:03:30,612 --> 00:03:43,024
Alex: A twenty-four-part growth teardown of Cal A I's onboarding flow, why one case study people remember keeps selling for months, and a security alert from OpenCode.

34
00:03:43,416 --> 00:03:57,554
Maya: 简单说：今天聊被 A I 引用为什么不等于被说对、当生产变便宜后真正的瓶颈挪到了哪里，以及为什么永远别把发送键交给机器人。

35
00:04:00,404 --> 00:04:02,471
Alex: Segment one. Top stories.

36
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:06,726
Maya: Our first story connects straight to our cold open.

37
00:04:06,726 --> 00:04:13,536
Maya: It comes from an account called heynavtoor, with about one hundred fifty-two thousand followers on X.

38
00:04:14,145 --> 00:04:18,970
Alex: He shared a study from three researchers at Washington University in Saint Louis.

39
00:04:18,970 --> 00:04:25,279
Alex: Now, let's be upfront: the numbers we have come from the X post summarizing the study.

40
00:04:25,279 --> 00:04:28,619
Alex: We did not read the original research paper itself.

41
00:04:29,194 --> 00:04:29,643
Maya: Right.

42
00:04:29,643 --> 00:04:37,198
Maya: According to the post, those three researchers ran searches on Google's own trending topics for forty days.

43
00:04:37,198 --> 00:04:47,270
Maya: They collected every single source cited inside Google's A I Overviews—those A I summary boxes at the top of the search results.

44
00:04:47,918 --> 00:04:51,301
Alex: And how did they test whether the A I was telling the truth?

45
00:04:51,794 --> 00:04:56,897
Maya: They broke each A I summary down into individual claims, sentence by sentence.

46
00:04:56,897 --> 00:05:04,748
Maya: Then they checked each claim one by one against the actual web page that Google linked to as its source.

47
00:05:05,230 --> 00:05:06,249
Alex: And what did they find?

48
00:05:06,822 --> 00:05:16,591
Maya: About eleven percent—roughly one in nine—of the claims in Google's A I Overviews had no basis in the cited web page.

49
00:05:16,591 --> 00:05:21,476
Maya: And in some cases, the cited page actually said the exact opposite.

50
00:05:21,935 --> 00:05:26,936
Alex: Wait a second. So the footnote link is real. It's not a broken link.

51
00:05:27,393 --> 00:05:33,126
Maya: No, the footnote is completely real! The web page exists. The link works.

52
00:05:33,126 --> 00:05:39,300
Maya: But what Google's A I claims that page says is not necessarily true.

53
00:05:39,853 --> 00:05:51,516
Alex: That is a huge wake-up call for anyone working on G E O—Generative Engine Optimization, which is how you get A I search engines and assistants to cite your site.

54
00:05:52,059 --> 00:05:58,161
Maya: Because most people think the game ends the moment the A I puts your link in the summary.

55
00:05:58,161 --> 00:06:04,602
Maya: This study shows that's only half the battle. The A I can cite you and still misquote you.

56
00:06:05,336 --> 00:06:18,479
Maya: 也就是说：脚注里的链接是真的，但 Google 的 A I 总结出来的话，有大概九分之一在原网页里根本找不到依据，甚至完全相反。

57
00:06:19,055 --> 00:06:21,918
Alex: Why does this matter so much to Fisher's work?

58
00:06:22,471 --> 00:06:33,489
Maya: Because if an A I summarizes a travel guide or a product page and gets a core fact backwards, the user gets bad advice, even while looking at your brand name in the footnote.

59
00:06:34,038 --> 00:06:38,087
Alex: So how do you defend against an A I misreading your page?

60
00:06:38,586 --> 00:06:48,010
Maya: The clearer your key facts are written, and the easier they are to verify on the page, the smaller the chance the A I gets them wrong.

61
00:06:48,010 --> 00:06:54,292
Maya: And that maps directly to Fisher's library item D R oh three, plus D R oh seven.

62
00:06:54,745 --> 00:06:58,943
Alex: Remind us what D R oh three and D R oh seven are.

63
00:06:59,432 --> 00:07:10,499
Maya: D R oh three is Fisher's rule that at the bottom of every page, you write down how the facts were verified and the exact date they were checked, along with the official source.

64
00:07:10,499 --> 00:07:16,190
Maya: And D R oh seven is the rule that A I only cites pages it dares to trust.

65
00:07:16,779 --> 00:07:29,826
Alex: So for a writing angle, the daily notes suggest combining D R oh three and D R oh seven around the theme: "When A I cites you, that doesn't mean A I got you right."

66
00:07:30,316 --> 00:07:40,469
Maya: And remember our hard rule here: if Fisher writes that post, he should use only general examples and never include absolute traffic numbers from YouChina.

67
00:07:40,469 --> 00:07:44,936
Maya: And we have a reply draft for heynavtoor in Segment four.

68
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:57,823
Alex: So the takeaway is: getting into the A I footnote is only step one; stating your facts and verification dates clearly is what keeps the A I from misquoting you.

69
00:07:58,744 --> 00:08:07,564
Maya: 一句话：被 A I 引用不等于被说对，把关键事实和核实日期写清楚，才不容易被传错。

70
00:08:10,414 --> 00:08:18,721
Alex: Let's pair that right away with story seven, from Peter Yang, who has about two hundred eighty-eight thousand followers on X.

71
00:08:19,337 --> 00:08:27,877
Maya: Peter published a new article asking a provocative question: "Is software dead, or is it just not built for humans anymore?"

72
00:08:28,358 --> 00:08:32,522
Alex: Okay, that title grabs your attention. How did he break it down?

73
00:08:33,025 --> 00:08:36,237
Maya: In the article, he gave four arguments on each side.

74
00:08:36,237 --> 00:08:44,587
Maya: Because it was a long post, our A P I read only captured the first part, so we will stick strictly to what we actually read.

75
00:08:45,213 --> 00:08:46,482
Alex: What was in the part we read?

76
00:08:47,034 --> 00:08:59,239
Maya: First, he pointed out that to replace a S a a S tool—that's software as a service, tools you pay a monthly or yearly subscription for—you don't need to rebuild the whole product.

77
00:08:59,239 --> 00:09:03,856
Maya: You only need to copy the twenty percent of features that you actually use.

78
00:09:04,476 --> 00:09:06,590
Alex: Did he give a real example of doing that?

79
00:09:07,167 --> 00:09:07,551
Maya: Yes.

80
00:09:07,551 --> 00:09:16,852
Maya: He said he had Claude build a small feature in five minutes that replaced a YouTube tool he was paying two hundred dollars a year for.

81
00:09:17,451 --> 00:09:21,203
Alex: Five minutes to replace a two-hundred-dollar-a-year subscription.

82
00:09:21,203 --> 00:09:22,908
Alex: What were his next points?

83
00:09:23,377 --> 00:09:29,577
Maya: His second point is that A I agents are replacing humans as the primary users of software.

84
00:09:29,577 --> 00:09:37,966
Maya: And his third point balances it out: while building the initial software is easy now, maintaining it and supporting customers is still hard.

85
00:09:37,966 --> 00:09:43,802
Maya: The rest of his post after that was cut off in the A P I read.

86
00:09:44,361 --> 00:09:47,128
Alex: Did Peter Yang have a second post in the feed today?

87
00:09:47,679 --> 00:09:48,423
Maya: He did.

88
00:09:48,423 --> 00:09:56,601
Maya: He shared a prompt you can use to have an A I audit the third-party apps connected to your Google account.

89
00:09:56,601 --> 00:09:59,203
Maya: And notice the instruction inside that prompt.

90
00:09:59,203 --> 00:10:05,151
Maya: Roughly: show me your recommendations before changing anything, and only disconnect the ones I explicitly approve.

91
00:10:05,776 --> 00:10:11,474
Alex: Hey, look at that rule! Show me first, and only touch what I clearly approve.

92
00:10:11,474 --> 00:10:16,413
Alex: That is the exact same safety mindset Fisher uses with his own assistants.

93
00:10:16,935 --> 00:10:30,483
Maya: 简单说：软件的主要用户正在从人变成 Agent；而且哪怕让 A I 去查账号权限，也得规定“先给建议、我点头才准动手”。

94
00:10:31,052 --> 00:10:47,863
Alex: And for a writing angle on Peter's main article, the daily notes point to Fisher's library item E V oh two—where on his site with zero ad spend, A I assistants are already the third largest traffic source—combined with D R oh seven.

95
00:10:48,325 --> 00:10:56,723
Maya: The angle is: "In the future, your pages will be read by agents first." As always, no absolute numbers in public.

96
00:10:56,723 --> 00:11:06,322
Maya: Peter's full article is worth Fisher reading when he has time, and we have a sharp reply draft for Peter in Segment four.

97
00:11:06,849 --> 00:11:14,129
Alex: So the takeaway is: write your pages for an agent reader that just wants clean answers to small questions.

98
00:11:15,091 --> 00:11:23,106
Maya: 一句话：当 Agent 成了主要读者，网页就得写成一节回答一个清楚的小问题。

99
00:11:25,956 --> 00:11:32,949
Alex: And if agents really are becoming the main users of the web, who wins and who loses?

100
00:11:32,949 --> 00:11:44,878
Alex: That brings us to story eight, from an account called j s p u j j i, based in Saint Louis, with about one hundred eighty-two thousand followers.

101
00:11:45,358 --> 00:11:53,788
Maya: He posted a chart laying out the winners, losers, and semi-losers from what he calls the agent-ification of the internet.

102
00:11:53,788 --> 00:11:59,919
Maya: Keep in mind, this is purely his personal opinion—there is no data attached to it.

103
00:12:00,532 --> 00:12:04,478
Alex: Walk us through his three columns. Who did he put in the winners column?

104
00:12:05,004 --> 00:12:08,985
Maya: Businesses with physical goods or offline capabilities.

105
00:12:08,985 --> 00:12:15,240
Maya: Restaurants, real estate, supermarkets, small e-commerce shops, and actual hotels.

106
00:12:15,886 --> 00:12:21,132
Alex: Because an A I agent cannot cook your dinner or give you a physical bed to sleep in.

107
00:12:21,132 --> 00:12:23,755
Alex: Who did he put in the semi-losers column?

108
00:12:24,323 --> 00:12:30,338
Maya: Companies where the front end is online, but the back end is physical logistics.

109
00:12:30,338 --> 00:12:32,916
Maya: He named Amazon, DoorDash, and Instacart.

110
00:12:33,487 --> 00:12:35,418
Alex: And who is in his full losers column?

111
00:12:35,996 --> 00:12:37,584
Maya: Middlemen and aggregators.

112
00:12:37,584 --> 00:12:49,231
Maya: Specifically, O T A s—online travel agencies like Expedia—along with Airbnb, price comparison sites, and content or lead-generation businesses.

113
00:12:49,231 --> 00:12:55,584
Maya: His blunt conclusion is: "The front end of the internet is dead."

114
00:12:56,170 --> 00:12:58,841
Alex: Hold on. Let's pause right there.

115
00:12:58,841 --> 00:13:03,420
Alex: Content sites and travel sites are sitting right in his losers column.

116
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:04,598
Maya: They are.

117
00:13:04,598 --> 00:13:10,177
Maya: Which puts a travel content site like Fisher's right in the crosshairs of this argument.

118
00:13:10,715 --> 00:13:14,549
Alex: So does Fisher agree that all travel content sites get wiped out?

119
00:13:15,046 --> 00:13:20,719
Maya: No, and this is where Fisher's library item D R oh six comes in.

120
00:13:20,719 --> 00:13:27,525
Maya: D R oh six says that for a travel site, being verifiable matters more than just ranking high.

121
00:13:28,055 --> 00:13:31,532
Alex: Let's separate the two kinds of travel pages.

122
00:13:31,532 --> 00:13:39,645
Alex: If a site just compares prices, or rewrites generic lists from other blogs, an A I agent can replace that easily.

123
00:13:40,114 --> 00:13:42,567
Maya: Exactly. Price comparison gets eaten first.

124
00:13:42,567 --> 00:13:50,333
Maya: But what about local information on the ground that a real person actually checked, and put a date on?

125
00:13:50,333 --> 00:13:54,012
Maya: That is much harder for an agent to replace.

126
00:13:54,756 --> 00:14:07,593
Maya: 也就是说：纯比价和拼凑的内容站会被 Agent 吃掉，但有人真正核实过、标了日期的一手当地信息，机器没法凭空替代。

127
00:14:08,086 --> 00:14:20,119
Alex: For writing, the daily notes say don't write a standalone post on this—just reply directly to j s p u j j i using that angle from D R oh six.

128
00:14:20,119 --> 00:14:29,599
Alex: And there's a note to be careful about how closely Fisher links his personal X account to YouChina, which we'll cover in Segment four.

129
00:14:30,183 --> 00:14:39,496
Maya: So the takeaway is: don't build a middleman page that just compares prices; build the verified local source that an agent has to rely on.

130
00:14:40,706 --> 00:14:48,847
Maya: 一句话：比价信息最容易被替代，真去核实过的一手当地信息才是护城河。

131
00:14:51,697 --> 00:14:56,707
Alex: Segment two. A I workflows, bottlenecks, and human-in-the-loop rules.

132
00:14:57,331 --> 00:14:59,742
Maya: Story two is a big one.

133
00:14:59,742 --> 00:15:08,581
Maya: It comes from François Chollet, a well-known A I researcher with about seven hundred forty-eight thousand followers on X.

134
00:15:08,581 --> 00:15:11,796
Maya: His post got four hundred seventy-four bookmarks.

135
00:15:12,378 --> 00:15:18,599
Alex: He shared four observations about what he put in quotes as the "total" automation of software engineering.

136
00:15:18,599 --> 00:15:20,795
Alex: Let's go through all four.

137
00:15:21,271 --> 00:15:29,213
Maya: First: there are more software engineers today than there were in twenty twenty-five, and demand for them is still rising.

138
00:15:29,832 --> 00:15:35,602
Alex: Wait—people predicted software engineers would disappear! Instead, there are more of them.

139
00:15:35,602 --> 00:15:37,378
Alex: What's observation two?

140
00:15:38,017 --> 00:15:41,438
Maya: Second: they have never been more exhausted.

141
00:15:42,069 --> 00:15:47,992
Alex: More engineers, and they're more tired than ever. Why? What's observation three?

142
00:15:48,556 --> 00:15:51,739
Maya: Third: they barely write code by hand anymore.

143
00:15:51,739 --> 00:15:56,115
Maya: Almost all of the code is written by A I agents.

144
00:15:56,594 --> 00:16:04,757
Alex: Okay, so agents write almost all the code, engineers are exhausted, and what happens to the companies employing them?

145
00:16:04,757 --> 00:16:06,476
Alex: That's observation four.

146
00:16:06,964 --> 00:16:13,792
Maya: Fourth: for the most part, the profits of the companies hiring them have not improved from using A I.

147
00:16:14,427 --> 00:16:19,012
Alex: Let's put those four pieces together. Agents write the code.

148
00:16:19,012 --> 00:16:24,015
Alex: Engineers are working harder than ever. And company profits haven't budged.

149
00:16:24,015 --> 00:16:26,516
Alex: How does Chollet explain that paradox?

150
00:16:27,076 --> 00:16:29,798
Maya: He says you have to think in systems.

151
00:16:29,798 --> 00:16:35,581
Maya: When you automate one step of a process, you create new friction somewhere else in the system.

152
00:16:35,581 --> 00:16:39,663
Maya: The bottleneck didn't vanish. It just moved to a new spot.

153
00:16:40,149 --> 00:16:45,947
Alex: And two other accounts in the feed touched on the same theme last night.

154
00:16:45,947 --> 00:16:53,400
Alex: An account called staysaasy, with seventeen thousand followers, noted that bug rates across the industry are going down.

155
00:16:54,027 --> 00:17:06,157
Maya: Meanwhile, an account called e p t w t s, with one hundred thirty-nine thousand followers, pointed out that building a good product that thousands of people actually use is still very hard.

156
00:17:06,157 --> 00:17:14,362
Maya: You still have to know what you want to build, understand user experience, and go back and forth with the agent many times.

157
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:26,306
Maya: 简单说：把写代码自动化之后，公司的利润并没暴涨，因为系统里的卡点只是从“生产”挪到了别的地方。

158
00:17:26,869 --> 00:17:29,334
Alex: Now remember, Fisher does not write code.

159
00:17:29,334 --> 00:17:33,206
Alex: Why does François Chollet's point hit home for Fisher?

160
00:17:33,731 --> 00:17:40,526
Maya: Because Fisher has worked in growth for over a decade, and this is the oldest law in growth!

161
00:17:40,526 --> 00:17:46,944
Maya: When A I makes building pages and writing content cheap and fast, the bottleneck doesn't disappear.

162
00:17:46,944 --> 00:17:49,209
Maya: It moves to distribution and trust.

163
00:17:49,741 --> 00:17:52,966
Alex: Anyone can make a lot of pages fast now.

164
00:17:52,966 --> 00:17:58,699
Alex: Getting the right people to find those pages and actually trust them is harder than ever.

165
00:17:59,197 --> 00:18:08,960
Maya: That connects directly to Fisher's library item W M P five c—the core growth formula, and which variable changed in the A I era.

166
00:18:08,960 --> 00:18:17,971
Maya: That's our backup idea for an original post today, and a great angle for replying to Chollet without ever claiming Fisher writes code.

167
00:18:18,429 --> 00:18:29,315
Alex: So the takeaway is: when A I makes production cheap, stop obsessing over production speed and focus on where the bottleneck moved—distribution and trust.

168
00:18:30,349 --> 00:18:38,361
Maya: 一句话：当 A I 把生产变便宜，真正的瓶颈就挪到了分发和信任上。

169
00:18:41,211 --> 00:18:48,022
Alex: Story three comes from Eric Siu in Los Angeles, who has about forty-six thousand followers.

170
00:18:48,022 --> 00:18:52,705
Alex: And this story inspired today's number one original post draft.

171
00:18:53,248 --> 00:19:05,712
Maya: Eric posted about his "Outreach Agent." Every single day, this bot lines up a small batch of warm outreach email drafts—emails to people he already knows or has a connection with.

172
00:19:06,271 --> 00:19:07,967
Alex: How does the bot pick who to email?

173
00:19:08,481 --> 00:19:14,345
Maya: It only keeps the high-confidence ones, and it attaches the reasoning to every single draft.

174
00:19:14,345 --> 00:19:21,675
Maya: And then—here is the key line—it waits for the boss to approve the batch before anything goes out.

175
00:19:22,275 --> 00:19:27,874
Alex: In Eric's words: his Outreach Agent has never sent a single email on its own.

176
00:19:28,329 --> 00:19:36,660
Maya: He said he built it for founders and salespeople who want help with outreach, but do not want to hand the "send" button over to a bot.

177
00:19:36,660 --> 00:19:45,917
Maya: He also had a second post about a skill he built to run his YouTube channel—packaging, editing, and uploading—which he rated a seven point five out of ten.

178
00:19:46,441 --> 00:19:50,764
Alex: Let's talk about that first post: never handing the send button to a bot.

179
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:55,681
Maya: That is literally the exact hard rule of Fisher's own assistant team!

180
00:19:55,681 --> 00:20:01,171
Maya: In Fisher's setup, his bots research, draft, and check each other's work every day.

181
00:20:01,171 --> 00:20:09,063
Maya: Not a single one of them is allowed to publish a post, send a reply, or send an email to an outside person.

182
00:20:09,538 --> 00:20:17,826
Alex: Seeing someone like Eric Siu pitch "the bot never presses send" as a selling point to founders is great validation.

183
00:20:17,826 --> 00:20:26,114
Alex: It shows that keeping the send button in human hands isn't being slow or timid—it's smart operations.

184
00:20:26,560 --> 00:20:38,836
Maya: 也就是说：让助手每天准备好带依据的草稿、但绝不把发送键交给 Bot，不是保守，外面已经有人把它当卖点来讲了。

185
00:20:39,417 --> 00:20:54,225
Alex: For writing, the daily notes anchor this on library item E V oh eight—Fisher's three-bot system and error correction log—and rule D R oh five, which holds the rules for his A I assistants.

186
00:20:54,761 --> 00:21:04,946
Maya: It also works as another clean, everyday example for version three of Fisher's C R M article, D R oh eight: the bot prepares, the human decides.

187
00:21:04,946 --> 00:21:19,495
Maya: And because we picked this theme for today's original post, we will talk in Segment four about whether to post the original or reply to Eric, so we don't repeat the exact same point twice in one morning.

188
00:21:20,050 --> 00:21:26,437
Alex: So the takeaway is: let your agents do the prep work, and keep the send button for yourself.

189
00:21:27,470 --> 00:21:35,671
Maya: 一句话：草稿写坏了随时能改，消息发出去收不回来，发送键必须留给人。

190
00:21:38,521 --> 00:21:42,928
Alex: Speaking of agents sending emails, let's look at story ten.

191
00:21:42,928 --> 00:21:58,155
Alex: This pairs a post from t h s o t t i a u x, who has about eight hundred eighty-six thousand followers, with two posts from Ben Tossell, who has about two hundred two thousand followers.

192
00:21:58,673 --> 00:22:03,811
Maya: Let's start with t h s o t t i a u x.

193
00:22:03,811 --> 00:22:08,582
Maya: He shared that his work agent coordinated with his personal agent over email.

194
00:22:09,150 --> 00:22:13,466
Alex: Wait. His work agent emailed his personal agent? Why?

195
00:22:14,052 --> 00:22:21,849
Maya: To request documents that the personal agent had access to, and then coordinate with his human accountant to file his taxes!

196
00:22:21,849 --> 00:22:24,077
Maya: The whole thing took twenty minutes.

197
00:22:24,077 --> 00:22:28,161
Maya: He ended the post by saying, roughly: we're still early.

198
00:22:28,682 --> 00:22:32,277
Alex: And Ben Tossell jumped in on that theme too.

199
00:22:32,277 --> 00:22:37,868
Alex: Yesterday we covered Ben's post about his own bot not checking its email.

200
00:22:37,868 --> 00:22:48,650
Alex: Today Ben posted that when agents email each other, they are super polite, and that email threads are actually a great format for agent-to-agent conversations.

201
00:22:49,204 --> 00:22:59,666
Maya: Ben also had a second post saying a whole generation of new product builders won't even know what a P R review is—that's a pull request review in software engineering.

202
00:23:00,269 --> 00:23:06,055
Alex: Let's look at why email threads work for those agents, and how Fisher's assistants talk to each other.

203
00:23:06,687 --> 00:23:10,731
Maya: Fisher's assistants don't send emails back and forth.

204
00:23:10,731 --> 00:23:20,841
Maya: Instead, as recorded in library item E V oh eight, they talk to each other through shared group log files and a blackboard ticket system.

205
00:23:21,322 --> 00:23:32,100
Alex: Whether you use an email thread like t h s o t t i a u x or a shared log file and ticket board like Fisher, what do they have in common?

206
00:23:32,736 --> 00:23:36,347
Maya: Every single step leaves a written record that you can audit later!

207
00:23:36,898 --> 00:23:49,834
Maya: 简单说：不管是让 Agent 互发邮件，还是像 Fisher 的团队那样用共享日志和工单板，核心都一样——每一步交接都必须留文字记录。

208
00:23:50,435 --> 00:23:55,234
Alex: The daily notes say no need to write a standalone post here.

209
00:23:55,234 --> 00:24:07,232
Alex: Since Fisher replied to Ben Tossell yesterday, skip replying to Ben today and use this angle to reply to t h s o t t i a u x instead.

210
00:24:07,844 --> 00:24:15,801
Maya: So the takeaway is: make your agents communicate in writing so every handoff leaves a trail you can inspect.

211
00:24:16,978 --> 00:24:24,349
Maya: 一句话：助手之间用文字留痕沟通，出问题时每一步都有账可查。

212
00:24:27,199 --> 00:24:32,818
Alex: Once you have a written trail of mistakes, you start adding rules to your system.

213
00:24:32,818 --> 00:24:42,556
Alex: And that leads right into story nine, from m v r c k h c k r, a small account with three hundred forty-two followers.

214
00:24:42,556 --> 00:24:45,928
Alex: Fisher replied to a different post of his yesterday.

215
00:24:46,461 --> 00:24:50,033
Maya: Today he posted a short thread about rules inside companies.

216
00:24:50,033 --> 00:25:02,534
Maya: He started by saying that a company that runs on clear rules is the fairest place for an average person, because the system answers questions nobody should expect an individual to guess on their own.

217
00:25:03,147 --> 00:25:04,335
Alex: That makes sense.

218
00:25:04,335 --> 00:25:10,674
Alex: What happens in companies where rules and personal judgment get mixed up in a messy way?

219
00:25:11,127 --> 00:25:18,061
Maya: In those companies, whenever one person makes a mistake, management slaps a brand-new rule onto everyone.

220
00:25:18,061 --> 00:25:27,442
Maya: Then, when the next unexpected situation happens and there's no rule for it, the blame still falls right back on the worker.

221
00:25:28,017 --> 00:25:33,320
Alex: And then he ended with a question that really makes you stop and think. What did he ask?

222
00:25:33,929 --> 00:25:38,194
Maya: He asked: "Look at the last rule you added after a mistake.

223
00:25:38,194 --> 00:25:43,170
Maya: Does it answer the next problem, or does it only answer the last one?"

224
00:25:43,723 --> 00:25:46,034
Alex: Oh, that is such a good question.

225
00:25:46,034 --> 00:25:50,325
Alex: "Does your new rule answer the next problem, or only the last one?"

226
00:25:50,920 --> 00:25:55,679
Maya: Think about Fisher's own folder of assistant rules and his correction log.

227
00:25:55,679 --> 00:25:58,607
Maya: That's library item D R oh five.

228
00:25:58,607 --> 00:26:09,590
Maya: Many rules in there—like "every file has only one writer" or "if I don't reply, that is not a yes"—was added right after a real mistake happened.

229
00:26:10,185 --> 00:26:22,946
Maya: 也就是说：每次出错后加新规矩时，都要问自己一句——这条规矩到底能不能防住下一类问题，还是只给上一次的失误打了个补丁？

230
00:26:23,436 --> 00:26:30,759
Alex: The daily notes say don't write a standalone post on this, just use D R oh five to reply to him.

231
00:26:30,759 --> 00:26:41,743
Alex: Even though he's a small account and we replied to another post of his yesterday, this is the only small account on our reply list today, and the angle is completely different.

232
00:26:42,266 --> 00:26:50,798
Maya: So the takeaway is: audit your rulebook to make sure your rules prevent whole categories of mistakes, not just yesterday's accident.

233
00:26:51,984 --> 00:26:59,227
Maya: 一句话：好规矩管的是一类问题，而不是只管上一次犯过的那个错。

234
00:27:02,077 --> 00:27:02,862
Maya: Quick break.

235
00:27:02,862 --> 00:27:20,126
Maya: When we come back: a twenty-four-part teardown of Cal A I's onboarding flow, why one case study brought in multiple six-figure deals over ten months, seven rules to stop Claude from sounding like a robot, and an OpenCode security alert.

236
00:27:20,746 --> 00:27:33,676
Maya: 休息一下，马上回来聊 Cal A I 把注册放到付费墙之后的增长细节、怎么让案例替你卖货，以及几条实用的内容与安全提醒。

237
00:27:38,026 --> 00:27:44,725
Alex: Welcome back. Segment three. Growth teardowns, writing rules, and quick hits.

238
00:27:45,365 --> 00:27:48,876
Maya: Let's kick off Segment three with story five.

239
00:27:48,876 --> 00:27:55,900
Maya: It comes from Alice Ercolani, based in San Marino, who has one thousand three hundred sixty-three followers.

240
00:27:55,900 --> 00:28:03,313
Maya: She wrote a twenty-four-post thread breaking down the growth mechanics of an app called Cal A I.

241
00:28:03,878 --> 00:28:07,642
Alex: Cal A I is an A I calorie-tracking app.

242
00:28:07,642 --> 00:28:16,298
Alex: Her post mentions it makes four hundred thousand dollars a month, though as our notes point out, that number is not independently verified.

243
00:28:16,298 --> 00:28:19,310
Alex: What did she find inside its onboarding flow?

244
00:28:19,828 --> 00:28:25,950
Maya: Every single step is a classic growth technique applied to an A I subscription app.

245
00:28:25,950 --> 00:28:31,256
Maya: First: the welcome screen has just one sentence of value and one button.

246
00:28:31,760 --> 00:28:34,735
Alex: Simple start. What happens after you tap the button?

247
00:28:35,375 --> 00:28:44,744
Maya: Second: it takes you through a long questionnaire, and on every question it reminds you that your answers are being used to build your custom plan.

248
00:28:44,744 --> 00:28:51,591
Maya: Third: right when the user hits their peak moment of excitement, the app asks for an app store rating.

249
00:28:52,130 --> 00:28:55,256
Alex: Catching people at the exact moment they feel good.

250
00:28:55,256 --> 00:28:58,034
Alex: What about the free trial and the paywall?

251
00:28:58,680 --> 00:29:10,231
Maya: Fourth: before asking for money, it shows a super clear three-day trial timeline and promises to send a reminder before charging you, which lowers trial anxiety.

252
00:29:10,231 --> 00:29:19,214
Maya: And fifth—here is the standout move in post seventeen of twenty-four—it delays account creation until after the paywall!

253
00:29:19,767 --> 00:29:20,162
Alex: Wait.

254
00:29:20,162 --> 00:29:27,894
Alex: You go through the quiz, see your plan, hit the paywall, start your trial, and only create an account after that?

255
00:29:28,449 --> 00:29:28,888
Maya: Yes!

256
00:29:28,888 --> 00:29:35,866
Maya: Because asking people to register an email and password upfront is where the biggest drop-off happens.

257
00:29:35,866 --> 00:29:50,232
Maya: She also noted two more details: every user gets a referral code that gives five dollars for inviting a friend, and their A S O—App Store Optimization—puts the main keywords near the front.

258
00:29:50,962 --> 00:30:02,683
Maya: 简单说：Cal A I 把最容易流失用户的“注册账号”放到了付费墙之后，让用户先看到定制方案的价值，再动手注册。

259
00:30:03,282 --> 00:30:08,692
Alex: Why did that specific detail—"put registration at the end"—jump out for Fisher?

260
00:30:09,258 --> 00:30:18,342
Maya: Because it matches Fisher's own painful product lesson in library item W M P four b: "Seven users, thirty-one days no signup.

261
00:30:18,342 --> 00:30:30,833
Maya: What I changed." When nobody is signing up for your product, maybe the problem isn't the product—maybe you put the registration wall way too early, before the user got any value.

262
00:30:31,368 --> 00:30:48,126
Alex: So for a writing angle, the daily notes suggest pairing W M P four b—or W M P five a, about whether membership systems still work in A I subscriptions—with public examples like Cal A I.

263
00:30:48,126 --> 00:30:58,709
Alex: The theme: "When nobody signs up, maybe you asked them to sign up too early." And remember: use public examples only, no personal numbers.

264
00:30:59,335 --> 00:31:08,874
Maya: Plus, because Alice's account has just over a thousand followers, a thoughtful reply on her seventeenth post is more likely to be seen.

265
00:31:09,407 --> 00:31:17,221
Alex: So the takeaway is: don't ask users to create an account before they have experienced what makes your product valuable.

266
00:31:18,261 --> 00:31:25,998
Maya: 一句话：注册是最容易赶走人的一步，先让用户尝到甜头，再让他注册。

267
00:31:28,848 --> 00:31:35,028
Alex: Story four is a follow-up from Marcel Kargul, who has about sixty thousand followers.

268
00:31:35,028 --> 00:31:41,209
Alex: Yesterday we covered his post saying going viral is not the same as getting clients.

269
00:31:41,672 --> 00:31:49,468
Maya: Today he shared a specific story about one single case study his team made for a client called sideshift.

270
00:31:49,468 --> 00:31:55,622
Maya: Again, the financial numbers here are his own claim on X and are not verified.

271
00:31:56,158 --> 00:31:59,401
Alex: What did he say that one case study did for his business?

272
00:31:59,955 --> 00:32:06,321
Maya: He said over the past ten months, that single case study brought them multiple six-figure client deals.

273
00:32:06,321 --> 00:32:16,579
Maya: And on almost every first sales call, the prospect says the exact same thing: "I saw what you did for sideshift, and I want that same level of quality."

274
00:32:17,219 --> 00:32:18,320
Alex: Think about that.

275
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:26,762
Alex: Ten months later, one well-documented project is still doing the selling for him before he even opens his mouth on a call.

276
00:32:27,262 --> 00:32:38,465
Maya: And the daily notes pair Marcel's post with a funny post from the same night by an account called p m i t u, who has twenty-seven thousand followers.

277
00:32:38,465 --> 00:32:42,079
Maya: His post got one thousand one hundred thirty-three likes.

278
00:32:42,079 --> 00:32:54,004
Maya: He joked about how the X algorithm works: post random nonsense and get five hundred thousand views; share two years of pouring your heart into a real product and get thirty-four views!

279
00:32:54,615 --> 00:32:58,055
Alex: Every builder on X has felt that exact pain.

280
00:32:58,055 --> 00:33:03,406
Alex: Thirty-four views on your real work, half a million views on a joke.

281
00:33:03,814 --> 00:33:17,268
Maya: 也就是说：在 X 上别被算法的随机流量带偏，只要把一两个真实案例讲透，让客户在通话时能脱口而出，它就会一直替你卖货。

282
00:33:17,861 --> 00:33:23,125
Alex: For Fisher, the notes say don't write a standalone post on this.

283
00:33:23,125 --> 00:33:36,082
Alex: Instead, use it to prioritize his own case study library: polish one or two real stories until they are ready to publish and easy for someone else to repeat in one sentence.

284
00:33:36,082 --> 00:33:40,941
Alex: And we have a fresh reply draft for Marcel in Segment four.

285
00:33:41,517 --> 00:33:49,621
Maya: So the takeaway is: a case study starts selling for you the moment a stranger can summarize it in one sentence on a call.

286
00:33:50,965 --> 00:33:59,397
Maya: 一句话：一个能让人记住并复述的精品案例，顶得上十个面面俱到的普通介绍。

287
00:34:02,247 --> 00:34:07,422
Alex: Next up is story six, about writing without sounding like a robot.

288
00:34:07,422 --> 00:34:16,479
Alex: This comes from an account called E X M seven seven seven seven, with about one hundred fifty-two thousand followers.

289
00:34:16,995 --> 00:34:25,058
Maya: He shared seven rules to make Claude write much more like a human, and the post picked up two hundred forty-four bookmarks.

290
00:34:25,058 --> 00:34:27,512
Maya: Let's run through the seven rules.

291
00:34:27,884 --> 00:34:28,523
Alex: Let's hear them.

292
00:34:29,013 --> 00:34:36,367
Maya: Rule one: tell it to write simply. Rule two: tell it to write as little as possible.

293
00:34:36,367 --> 00:34:45,017
Maya: Rule three: use contractions—like "don't" instead of "do not"—and small asides so it has a human voice.

294
00:34:45,017 --> 00:34:49,343
Maya: Rule four: one idea per sentence, one sentence per line.

295
00:34:49,803 --> 00:34:56,066
Alex: Hey, that sounds a lot like our own podcast rule! What are rules five, six, and seven?

296
00:34:56,559 --> 00:35:01,059
Maya: Rule five: talk like you are having coffee with a good friend.

297
00:35:01,059 --> 00:35:07,059
Maya: Rule six: don't make it write the whole article at once—go paragraph by paragraph.

298
00:35:07,059 --> 00:35:16,059
Maya: And rule seven: at the end, have a second, different model read the draft and point out any spots a reader wouldn't understand.

299
00:35:16,692 --> 00:35:26,590
Alex: He also had a second post saying top creators still choose Midjourney for images because it captures the vibe you want, not just technical parameters.

300
00:35:27,208 --> 00:35:31,476
Maya: Look at how closely his writing setup matches Fisher's team.

301
00:35:31,476 --> 00:35:38,847
Maya: First, Fisher has strict "no A I tone" rules where he lists the exact robotic phrases that are banned.

302
00:35:38,847 --> 00:35:50,099
Maya: And second, just like rule number seven, in Fisher's team Argon writes the first draft, and a separate assistant audits and edits it before Fisher ever sees it.

303
00:35:50,671 --> 00:36:02,263
Maya: 简单说：想去掉文案里的机器味，光说“写得像人”不够，得列出禁用的词句，再让第二个助手专门挑毛病。

304
00:36:02,834 --> 00:36:17,734
Alex: The daily notes say skip writing a standalone post on this, and instead reply to E X M seven seven seven seven sharing how a banned-phrase list plus a second reviewing assistant works in practice.

305
00:36:18,280 --> 00:36:27,709
Maya: So the takeaway is: pair a specific blacklist of A I phrases with a second agent whose only job is to read like a skeptical human.

306
00:36:28,978 --> 00:36:37,010
Maya: 一句话：把禁用词列成清单，再让另一个助手专门审稿，最能治好 A I 腔。

307
00:36:39,860 --> 00:36:51,635
Alex: That connects nicely to story eleven, a Chinese thread from an account called sujingshen, a product builder in Guangzhou with about six thousand four hundred ninety-three followers.

308
00:36:52,217 --> 00:36:59,674
Maya: He reviewed version two of a creator platform called Tutti and gave it a score of seventy out of one hundred.

309
00:36:59,674 --> 00:37:02,869
Maya: Then he explained where the missing thirty points are.

310
00:37:03,380 --> 00:37:05,321
Alex: What did he think was wrong with the platform?

311
00:37:05,963 --> 00:37:10,585
Maya: First, the page is too crowded and has no clear hierarchy.

312
00:37:10,585 --> 00:37:17,307
Maya: He said a creator platform homepage should immediately answer three questions: What campaigns can I join?

313
00:37:17,307 --> 00:37:23,189
Maya: What assets can I get? And is this campaign a good fit for me?

314
00:37:23,732 --> 00:37:26,786
Alex: Clear product thinking. What were his next two points?

315
00:37:27,361 --> 00:37:30,194
Maya: Second, the campaign rules are scattered everywhere.

316
00:37:30,194 --> 00:37:40,310
Maya: Instead of slapping another A I chat box onto the page, they should organize the rules into a living knowledge base that grows over time.

317
00:37:40,887 --> 00:37:47,492
Alex: Oh, I love that line: "Build a knowledge base, not another chat box." And his third point?

318
00:37:47,978 --> 00:38:06,848
Maya: Third, when a creator submits a draft, the platform should pre-review it first: check if it violates platform rules, check if it hits any brand-sensitive timing, and suggest where it could be stronger—while following one golden rule: "Keep my voice, just catch the risks."

319
00:38:07,311 --> 00:38:20,050
Alex: "Keep my voice, catch the risks." And "put the rules in a single knowledge base instead of making people ask a chat bot every time." That is the exact workflow Fisher built for his own content!

320
00:38:20,424 --> 00:38:34,546
Maya: 也就是说：与其让用户每次去问聊天框，不如把规则整理成一页随手能查的知识库，并在预审时做到“保留作者口吻、只抓合规风险”。

321
00:38:35,149 --> 00:38:45,411
Alex: The daily notes say don't write a standalone post here, and instead leave a helpful Chinese reply agreeing with the knowledge base and pre-review points.

322
00:38:46,024 --> 00:38:51,902
Maya: So the takeaway is: a living one-page rulebook beats a chat box every single time.

323
00:38:53,198 --> 00:39:00,640
Maya: 一句话：把规则写成一页不断更新的文档，比再加一个聊天框管用得多。

324
00:39:03,490 --> 00:39:13,205
Alex: Now for story twelve, an important security heads-up in Chinese from dotey, based in Chicago, with about two hundred sixty thousand followers.

325
00:39:13,677 --> 00:39:18,543
Maya: He shared an announcement that OpenCode put out on October seventeenth.

326
00:39:18,543 --> 00:39:26,063
Maya: The night before, OpenCode had a security incident affecting one point four percent of its Console users.

327
00:39:26,696 --> 00:39:29,400
Alex: How do you know if you are in that one point four percent?

328
00:39:30,011 --> 00:39:36,573
Maya: According to the announcement, as dotey relays it, if you did not receive an email from them, you were not affected.

329
00:39:37,198 --> 00:39:38,872
Alex: And what if someone did get the email?

330
00:39:39,427 --> 00:39:58,718
Maya: Dotey noted that according to media reports quoting the email—and let's be clear, this part comes from media reports, not from OpenCode's public post—third-party A P I keys stored inside affected workspaces, such as Open A I or Anthropic keys, may have been exposed.

331
00:39:58,718 --> 00:40:05,410
Maya: Those users need to revoke their old keys, generate new ones, and check their recent usage logs.

332
00:40:06,143 --> 00:40:18,708
Maya: 简单说：如果之前在 OpenCode Console 里存过第三方密钥，去查一下邮箱有没有收到通知；没用过或者没收到邮件就不用管。

333
00:40:19,168 --> 00:40:32,637
Alex: For Fisher, this is purely an operational check: if he or the team ever bound third-party provider keys inside OpenCode Console, check email; if not, skip right past it.

334
00:40:32,637 --> 00:40:35,331
Alex: No writing or replying needed here.

335
00:40:35,787 --> 00:40:42,131
Maya: So the takeaway is: check your inbox if you ever used OpenCode Console, and otherwise move on.

336
00:40:43,285 --> 00:40:49,818
Maya: 一句话：用过 OpenCode Console 就查下邮箱，没用过直接略过。

337
00:40:52,668 --> 00:40:56,693
Alex: And our final item, story thirteen, is marked borderline.

338
00:40:56,693 --> 00:41:02,507
Alex: It's from Luke Pierce in Miami, who has about fifteen thousand followers.

339
00:41:03,007 --> 00:41:13,961
